mpact":"89756829faabca8738248e042ff97429e4e8132a10cf448bdcc699a28d6219f3","nasal":"89756829faabca8738248e042ff97429e4e8132a10cf448bdcc699a28d6219f3"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-9-10","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":9,"verse":10,"english":"O Kaikeyī, ir Dandaka, situated towards the south, there is the city known by the name of Vaijayanta, where dwelt Timidhvaja, otherwise called Samvara, possessed of an hundred conjurations, and a mighty Asura. That unreproved gave battle Sakra one to accompanied by the celestials.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-9-12"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2009010","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"7375c2dcb9c46d62728ed19b3a3f8057522b2d299bea8a7e4a8e2bcd3b05f56a","compact":"69d5bc297e92e3909dcbf3c466a15e77b76ee452e901df7d8a2ada5245909e3c","nasal":"61c38d7b349a3236f88a476a2779401782b30e45d371463bc4db330806fe9dd4"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-9-11","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":9,"verse":11,"english":"O Kaikeyī, ir Dandaka, situated towards the south, there is the city known by the name of Vaijayanta, where dwelt Timidhvaja, otherwise called Samvara, possessed of an hundred conjurations, and a mighty Asura. That unreproved gave battle Sakra one to accompanied by the celestials.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.691,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-9-12"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2009011","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"204f0a47eb0eb802c61d692d4e24ff2b429915e5b4048fb4152e7db9ffb5bbbe","compact":"69941286bcbcf71791a3404161bef4fecb368ced33d826c8c1b24ea6b5eb7c01","nasal":"4de64ef56d28cb51f2b9e9264585d8a8e919796353630c3b487baa8dcc5b28ce"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-9-12","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":9,"verse":12,"english":"Then king Dasaratha warred with the Asuras most heroically. And that mighty armed one, O worshipful lady, losing his senses in consequence of wounds received from weapons, was removed from the field by you. In that imminent danger, your husband, sadly cut by weapons, was preserved by you.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.45,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-9-14"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2009012","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"6526b7131736cae5ea1de759739caa203e7271ca3917b9e41eefa954c26cebed","compact":"d113e9272644ef351af43f2f0d7f5e7d647baf8dd244e2e69a1dd28e25fa8acb","nasal":"bd6302592a76d88bb5331001f3da03000b5f0eeb63970877d231272ced497ee0"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-9-13","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":9,"verse":13,"english":"Then king Dasaratha warred with the Asuras most heroically. And that mighty armed one, O worshipful lady, losing his senses in consequence of wounds received from weapons, was removed from the field by you. In that imminent danger, your husband, sadly cut by weapons, was preserved by you. Thereupon gratified, he, O, you of gracious presence, granted you two boons. Whereupon you did say, 'I shall receive from my lord the boon whenever I shall wish. Thereupon that high-souled one said., 'So be it? I did not know anything about this, O respected one; and it was you who did formerly communicate this (to me.) And it is because I bear affection to you that I have not forgotten it. Now do you forcibly make the monarch desist from installing Rāma.","alignment":{"type":"split","confidence":0.529,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-9-14","DUTT-2-9-15"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2009013","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"7d0335d40edfe0a0d985a6ca1e454f426877dc3b97cc66877eea7a1e601a0578","compact":"1a487457c99830669e4d4f62ff2247895e34d61fa07ce42af1566d695680279d","nasal":"1a487457c99830669e4d4f62ff2247895e34d61fa07ce42af1566d695680279d"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-9-14","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":9,"verse":14,"english":"Thereupon gratified, he, O, you of gracious presence, granted you two boons. Whereupon you did say, 'I shall receive from my lord the boon whenever I shall wish. Thereupon that high-souled one said., 'So be it? I did not know anything about this, O respected one; and it was you who did formerly communicate this (to me.) And it is because I bear affection to you that I have not forgotten it. Now do you forcibly make the monarch desist from installing Rāma.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.656,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-9-15"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2009014","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"af00cb8f3b5f0a8cf3a2faafc43105b469cd79919a8749b499921469bbbd711a","compact":"126efa94352bfcc2d6b3e8bbb553d9408e273374457df3b131077a0866217a02","nasal":"126efa94352bfcc2d6b3e8bbb553d9408e273374457df3b131077a0866217a02"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-9-15","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":9,"verse":15,"english":"And ask your husband for these two boons, the installation of Bharata, and the exile of Rāma into the woods for fourteen years.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.569,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-9-16"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2009015","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"627a9aaaa610f4d741e921e216722a30103674fc5d91ce1b9eb5e973d252cc85","compact":"d7314e825635e1ece06e23fa9a8af2aefc82e19069ce4941d52b7453290b06f3","nasal":"27a3674e4726e56a94a0b512a51b8632cedf825960cda653c354785749c53e7e"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-9-16","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":9,"verse":16,"english":"Entering the anger-chamber to-day, do you, O daughter of Aśvapati, clad in soiled garment, lie down on the uncovered floor. Do not look at him, nor speak to him ought. Do you on beholding the lord of the earth, overwhelmed with grief, weep only.","alignment":{"type":"split","confidence":0.664,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-9-18","DUTT-2-9-19"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2009016","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"7a4661396469ddd95545e9e40c17d4d24beff860ae3959da05ab86675936c83f","compact":"2c12a6047773ad85231cfc740b81bb475796b2469f5729495270bbdef1f9f15a","nasal":"31e37f4acbe9a22ab59b98bc925c850d4301eca12737e0ce738e3073049ad0f5"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-9-17","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":9,"verse":17,"english":"You have always been the favourite wife of your husband. Of this I have not the least doubt, For your sake the monarch can enter into a flame.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-9-20"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2009017","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"739964f249915897f4a42967939ca8f1899f821e5b0bee6e42b4aac3689b964e","compact":"dbcfab89722432fb8cf36fc5e15f423fee40c93dda8d734c40a5d5fb565e9261","nasal":"dbcfab89722432fb8cf36fc5e15f423fee40c93dda8d734c40a5d5fb565e9261"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-9-18","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":9,"verse":18,"english":"He can never anger you, nor can he eye you when angered. For compassing your pleasure the king can renounce life itself.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.885,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-9-21"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2009018","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"78cee5cc83790b7c658a1474c96794fe3258bb6442af956427d1f42e2c5bfff4","compact":"92e4b5db2cd9a813e7a4190823e9bb0ec022fbc26b7b97f7cc57ce0d5c2dbaba","nasal":"92e4b5db2cd9a813e7a4190823e9bb0ec022fbc26b7b97f7cc57ce0d5c2dbaba"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-9-19","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":9,"verse":19,"english":"Therefore the monarch can never set aside your word. O senseless lady, do you now reflect upon the strength of your good fortune.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.81,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-9-22"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2009019","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"1c3cf1a9c7e9c3759333354ef992ff7ae37fd9d25505000f9989f7740896ad03","compact":"6d83529c0b42d9e002348bfd183468d9289e249e112aed8a9ae79393bf745ebd","nasal":"21e0f4ac8b2276bb4008324279efaa7da43ebb380c85f20975c59ad9b233dc39"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-9-20","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":9,"verse":20,"english":"King Dasaratha will offer you rubies and pearls and gold and gems of various kinds; but do not you bend your heart to them.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-9-23"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2009020","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"a4a2a232a4dfc5b55cbcdb266e11915a463b9fd4ebb9bae79808ad8f5991ff25","compact":"8b2e727c1f8c48d6d40653fcf7ae054beaad56f21f683412059b0a55baf85da4","nasal":"8b2e727c1f8c48d6d40653fcf7ae054beaad56f21f683412059b0a55baf85da4"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-9-21","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":9,"verse":21,"english":"Do you, O exalted dame, bring into Daśaratha's recollection the two boons which he had granted you at the time of the war between the gods and Asuras, and you shall not fail to achieve your objects.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.574,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-9-24"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2009021","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"e0e8e22edec62378274398ddbb2d7c7651b2e54a833d5327f7bec1a458ed3e1c","compact":"5ce555ba6e8aacd3c5c24a5d7279cbc64394d0d167ad21fe1a31d75590b319c5","nasal":"5ce555ba6e8aacd3c5c24a5d7279cbc64394d0d167ad21fe1a31d75590b319c5"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-9-22","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":9,"verse":22,"english":"And when that descendant of Raghu, raising you will go to bestow the boons, do you then binding him fast by oath, unfold to the monarch the boons, saying.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-9-25"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2009022","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"134f1643f5f1a0aa0dcaf67427b7158714b5a4352bca930a4eda58d5a82416e3","compact":"58980400753a2c1310398d3b4fa439361fe70b2ee312920a64c145bbf2e7b319","nasal":"58980400753a2c1310398d3b4fa439361fe70b2ee312920a64c145bbf2e7b319"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-9-23","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":9,"verse":23,"english":"Send Rāma to the forest for nine and five years, and let Bharata, becoming on earth the foremost of monarchs, carry on the Government.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.683,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-9-26"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2009023","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"efa7731e2f5c53ad3988fb24294ac9f413b53ba32f936260b61cca248ed3f430","compact":"5dfce17611966d076c6734962c21d012162a12e4a93bd84a296f3e0c8b206151","nasal":"18ba628a90c8ab63298d00be315a2ae4b43c06c844741726eda70145fa8815d8"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-9-24","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":9,"verse":24,"english":"Thus banished Rāma will no longer maintain possession of the hearts of the people; and your Bharata with his foes put out, will be the king.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.702,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-9-29"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2009024","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"d6b675d3a37a2992c535b0045deccf7cc29de64d5c5aede086d907bb7dd9bc68","compact":"e5bdd6e326be32cb302629a04a07dc5d984ebb778484889d8dbd3f951223438a","nasal":"e5bdd6e326be32cb302629a04a07dc5d984ebb778484889d8dbd3f951223438a"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-9-25","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":9,"verse":25,"english":"By the time that Rāma returns from the forest, your son, your prudent son along with his friends, securing the hearts of the people externally and internally, will have been firmly established on the throne.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.367,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-9-30"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2009025","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"8e0702d76f23a0236c6dda34f282c4cf8ecbab8cf963213afaa726966356c467","compact":"ce2f13666e71edcaf69abba206fa4b9327f4c89e934bf594d796c3fa8689cace","nasal":"ce2f13666e71edcaf69abba206fa4b9327f4c89e934bf594d796c3fa8689cace"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-9-26","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":9,"verse":26,"english":"Now is the time, I apprehend. Renouncing fear, do you forcibly make the monarch remove from his mind his intention of installing Rāma.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.619,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-9-31"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2009026","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"f8570352d1826235914770af743d69298d75c57b775051e184e5f0254f065187","compact":"1320da2c38b8f8db5571b0051fd53de9304f64eba11da841c55b9c53ee82157b","nasal":"95af62c25b256e86aa6d92c46e547050a1b706046833b5e918487b00fd2a58ce"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-9-27","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":9,"verse":27,"english":"Having been thus made to accept that for good which was really evil, Kaikeyi, desirous of obtaining the boons, was filled with 